A||||5||theogre||Testing a TPS.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||01-08-2000||02:10 PM||||<B>Patrick Asked in another thread,</B><P>Ogre, you've often mentioned replacement of the TPS as a precaution when it's got a few years on it. That's probably good advice, but what is the reason that using a mutimeter on it for testing purposes wouldn't work? It would seem to me if it was mechanically worn out that this would show up in the electrical values registered as the throttle was moved from idle to wide open. Yes,no?<P><B>Ogre responds:</B><P>This is why I often just replace these. Especially on high milage ones.<P>Testing this item is a pain and often not accurate. Remember your dealing with a device that measures very small changes, and outputs small fractions of a volt. Any jitter in the sensor will screw up the mixture. This sensor is one device the ECM cannot work around. You can run the car without almost any other sensor, but not this one. ((It may run really badly but it will run without most of the others.)) <P>A digital meter is often jitery. You can't tell for sure if the voltage is really changing when measuring small variable sources or if the meter is sitting on the boundry of a number. If a DVM uses damping to reduce this then it will also smoothe the sensor output like a analog meter will.<P>An analog meter is usually "damped" to protect the movment(pointer) and it also has inertia in the pointer that dampens small changes. This damping can often smoothe out the tiny fluttering in a failing TPS. By the time many analog meters shows a failing TPS it's got to be pretty bad.<P>An ECM scanner is a better test. With this you see more closly what the ECM sees. This is better but even that isn't always a sure test. If you don't test the sensor at a bunch of points then you could miss a bad spot in the curve. <P>You can't just run the sensor up and down. You have to move it a tiny bit and lock it in place. make sure that reading is steady and move to the next spot you will test. You also have to watch as you move from each spot to see that the output changes smootly. You have to work you way slowly from closed to WOT and back. Even then you want to do it a couple times at least.<P>If you do test it carefully and are happy with the results, then it may not be your problem at the moment. But keep in mind this device works by moving a contact along a resitor element. Like an old radio where the volume crackles as you move the knob this sensor will fail in the same ways. It is a wholely mechanical sensor and while it is fairly well sealed when new the seal does wear and will allow moisture and dirt in to accelerate wear or throw off the sensor value. Anytime you take the sensor off for inspection, which should be done every couple years at least, and see any wear between the rotor and the shell, the sensor should be replaced, even if it tests good. Remember we are dealing with tiny changes in output. Any contamination at all will mess it up.<P>Aditionally there is a spring inside the TPS used on the 700 TBI. I think the V6 and the older 300TBI (86 and down 4 Cyl) also have an internal spring. If the spring breaks or is weak the sensor won't work right.<P>The purpose of the spring is to keep the sensor rotor tight to the TBI shaft. This makes them move like one part. If the spring is weak or broken the rotor may not move untill the shaft has moved quite far. This of course scrags the mix since the rotor is now esentialy lieing to the ECM about the position of the TBI shaft.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||01-08-2000||04:04 PM||||<BR>Thanks Ogre, that's quite a comprehensive answer!||||reg||14
Z||000002||batboy||01-08-2000||11:28 PM||||What are some of the common symptoms of a poorly working TPS?||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-09-2000||12:46 AM||||You use an Ohmmeter to check a TPS sensor; this is the most accurate way. A break in the sensor won't show up with a voltmeter. Any break in the sensor will show unlimited resistance on the ohmmeter as you swipe the arm.<P>A TPS that dose not work at all will show the same symptoms that a faulty accelerator pump will on a carburated car. The car will fall flat on its face on acceleration and then start to go as the map sensor takes over. The map sensor serves the same function as a power valve does on a carburated car. It reads the engines vacuum signal so that the computer can add the appropriate fuel after the TPS has done its job. You have to use a good digital meter like a fluke 87 or 88 multi-meter to get accurate results.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||01-09-2000||02:09 PM||||I discovered my faulty TPS after noticing that my "up shift" light would come on when I was over 2000 RPM and would most often not go back out until I shifted up or let off the gas and waited for the engine to slow back down under 2000 RPM. I'm assuming that right before I replaced it, it had completely gone out since the car was trying to surge and jump while driving home with the new TPS in hand. IMO you should just change it when you begin to see effects of the sensor failing. No sense in spending money on something that might last quite a few more years.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-09-2000||02:12 PM||||An end to end resistance test is often meaningless. The resistor element isn't usually broken. <P>The wiper arm is the usual culprit. It's often either worn out/broken or contaminated. Even using a ohm test would require testing the wiper similar to the voltage test above. It also has to be done with the thing mounted because rotating it with a screw driver instead of the trottle shaft could change the alignment of the rotor giving a false test. (The above testing is done with the TPS mounted.... I negelected to mention that. I just assumed people would want to test in place.)<P>The resistor element can also be contaminated without being broken, or showing a change in total resistance. Contamination can either slightly shift resistance or insulate the wiper.||||reg||1
